**Reused evidence**
None from the earlier chapter dossiers applies cleanly to Chapter 6B, so no prior evidence IDs were reused.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria Poverty Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/nigeria/publication/afw-nigeria-poverty-assessment","claim":"The assessment introduced new analysis on Nigeria's labor market, climate shocks, and the role of social protection in poverty reduction, and argued that state-level data are crucial for designing poverty-reducing policy.","locator":"publication description paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank publication page summarizing the report's scope and policy use.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly relevant 2022 poverty-policy baseline and evidence environment.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly context rather than a direct Buhari action, but it frames the policy problem his administration faced.","contrary_evidence":["This is analytical context, not proof of implementation or ruler-specific effect."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria's need to spend more and better","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-11-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2022/11/21/nigeria-needs-to-spend-more-and-better","claim":"The World Bank said Nigeria spent only about 220 dollars per person in 2022, social sectors received less than a quarter of the national budget, and low public spending translated into poor development outcomes and weak human capital.","locator":"main body paragraphs around lines 230-234","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank feature with clear quantitative claims and policy interpretation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly describes 2022 spending conditions and their welfare implications.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Buhari's administration because it concerns federal public spending choices, though inherited fiscal constraints also mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The page is a policy diagnosis, not a direct audit of Buhari's personal management of each sector."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Budget Speech: Budget of Economic Growth and Sustainability – President Buhari","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2021-10-07","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/2022-budget-speech-budget-of-economic-growth-and-sustainability-president-buhari/","claim":"Buhari framed the 2022 budget around a vibrant, educated, and healthy populace, poverty reduction, and reduced regional and social disparities, and said MDAs were advised to use gender-responsive budgeting to reach vulnerable groups.","locator":"theme and priorities paragraphs 78-89","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official budget speech, but it is an intention-and-priority statement rather than a delivery report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 fiscal plan that governed the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler attribution for the budget proposal and stated priorities.","contrary_evidence":["Budget proposals do not establish actual delivery or beneficiary reach in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.3","6B.4","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Address By President Buhari At The 3rd Ministerial Performance Review Retreat","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-10-17","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/address-by-president-buhari-at-the-3rd-ministerial-performance-review-retreat/","claim":"Buhari said that by June 2022 the administration had enrolled 75 percent of Batch C N-Power beneficiaries, fed 9,990,862 pupils through the school-feeding programme, enrolled 988,652 poor and indigent Nigerians in BHCPF, accredited 7,373 primary health facilities, and launched a Presidential Delivery Tracker on 30 August 2022.","locator":"paragraphs 60-64 and 79-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official performance-review speech with specific delivery figures, though still self-reported by the administration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period implementation evidence from late 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari as head of the administration, but the figures reflect implementation by ministries and agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The numbers are self-reported and not independently audited in this source."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In Bid to Achieve Universal Coverage, President Buhari Signs National Health Authority Bill Into Law","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/in-bid-to-achieve-universal-coverage-president-buhari-signs-national-health-authority-bill-into-law/","claim":"Buhari signed the National Health Insurance Authority Bill 2022, said a fund would be created to cover 83 million poor Nigerians who cannot afford premiums, and said the new law would support digital records, data collection, and monitoring.","locator":"opening paragraphs and presidential remarks on the fund and digital records","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official enactment record with explicit institutional and coverage claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 social-policy action.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler action, although actual coverage depended on implementation by the Authority and state schemes.","contrary_evidence":["Signing the law did not itself prove that the fund or coverage was fully operational in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Humanitarian Issues: President Buhari Urges More Private Sector Participation, Highlights Intervention Programmes","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-09-23","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/humanitarian-issues-president-buhari-urges-more-private-sector-participation-highlights-intervention-programmes/","claim":"Buhari said the administration's humanitarian and social-development framework included the National IDP Policy, the National Emergency Flood and Response Plan, policies for the elderly and disability, the poverty-reduction plan, and social-investment programmes such as school feeding, conditional cash transfers, N-Power, and GEEP.","locator":"paragraphs 34-36","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official speech, but it is primarily self-reported policy framing and program enumeration.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 statement of welfare strategy and target groups.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler attribution for the administration's program agenda.","contrary_evidence":["The speech lists programs and goals but does not independently demonstrate national-scale impact."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Directs Humanitarian Affairs Ministry, Others to Ensure Unimpeded Return of IDPs in the North east","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-directs-humanitarian-affairs-ministry-others-to-ensure-unimpeded-return-of-idps-in-the-north-east/","claim":"Buhari directed ministries and agencies to develop programmes for explosive remnants, orphaned children, and return and rehabilitation of internally displaced people in the North East, and linked the directive to reducing the effects of insurgency and disaster.","locator":"paragraphs 28-35","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct target-year directive, but still a presidential instruction rather than proof of full delivery.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis-response evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari's crisis-response authority, though implementation depended on ministries, states, and security conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The underlying displacement and insurgency were inherited and not eliminated by this directive."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria Launches its Most Extensive National Measure of Multidimensional Poverty","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2022-11-17","url":"https://www.nigerianstat.gov.ng/news/78","claim":"The 2022 MPI survey found that 63 percent of Nigerians, about 133 million people, were multidimensionally poor; 65 percent of the poor lived in the North; rural poverty was 72 percent; and child poverty was even higher, with 67.5 percent of children multidimensionally poor.","locator":"press release highlights bullets","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official national statistical release with explicit survey methods and headline estimates.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Measures target-period welfare conditions and distribution during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly context and outcome, not a single-ruler causal estimate.","contrary_evidence":["The survey measures conditions rather than isolating Buhari's causal contribution."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New evidence on the situation of child deprivation in Nigeria","publisher":"UNICEF Nigeria","publication_date":"2022-05-27","url":"https://www.unicef.org/nigeria/press-releases/new-evidence-situation-child-deprivation-nigeria","claim":"UNICEF said about 54 percent of children in Nigeria were multidimensionally poor across nutrition, healthcare, education, water, sanitation, adequate housing, and information, with major rural and state disparities and a strong need for data-driven budgeting.","locator":"press release body and highlights around lines 59-68","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF press release summarizing jointly launched national reports with concrete child-poverty estimates.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly reflects 2022 child welfare conditions and policy priorities.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly context rather than direct Buhari attribution, but it informs the administration's welfare baseline and policy challenge.","contrary_evidence":["The report is diagnostic and does not by itself show federal implementation success or failure."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Water Sanitation and Hygiene National Outcome Routine Mapping Report 2021","publisher":"UNICEF Nigeria","publication_date":"2022-06","url":"https://www.unicef.org/nigeria/reports/water-sanitation-and-hygiene-national-outcome-routine-mapping-report-2021","claim":"The 2021 WASHNORM report, published in June 2022, found that about 48 million Nigerians still defecated in the open, only 8 percent practiced safe handwashing, and only 10 percent had access to basic WASH services combined, while also noting that routine measurement had become institutionalized by the government.","locator":"highlights paragraphs around lines 62-64","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF-hosted report page summarizing a government-partner survey with direct service-access figures.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Uses 2021 survey data published during the 2022 target year to describe the service baseline Buhari faced.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly baseline context rather than direct ruler credit, though the institutionalization of routine measurement is relevant to governance.","contrary_evidence":["The survey describes severe service deficits; it does not establish that Buhari corrected them in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"More than 1.5 million children at risk as devastating floods hit Nigeria","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/more-15-million-children-risk-devastating-floods-hit-nigeria","claim":"UNICEF said the 2022 floods left more than 2.5 million people needing humanitarian assistance, displaced 1.3 million people, killed over 600, and raised acute risks of waterborne disease, malnutrition, and learning disruption for children.","locator":"press release body around lines 195-201","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF emergency press release with concrete 2022 humanitarian-impact figures.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 crisis conditions affecting social well-being.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly crisis context, though the government response quality is relevant to Buhari's record.","contrary_evidence":["The flood emergency was driven by weather and climate shocks, not only federal policy."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria, Joint Evaluation of Social Protection for SDGs in Nigeria Project","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-07-06","url":"https://www.wfp.org/publications/nigeria-joint-evaluation-social-protection-sdgs-nigeria-project","claim":"The evaluation found that the UN-government social-protection programme alleviated recipients' financial burdens, encouraged them to seek professional health care, helped meet immediate needs, and strengthened coordination among government social-protection stakeholders.","locator":"summary findings bullets 1-5","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official evaluation summary of a joint UN-government programme with explicit outcome statements.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers January 2020 to June 2022, overlapping the target year and ending during Buhari's tenure.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Buhari's administration because it concerns a national social-protection programme, though implementation was joint with UN agencies and states.","contrary_evidence":["The evaluation is joint and does not isolate Buhari's personal contribution from partner agencies."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria's public university lecturers suspend strike after eight months","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-10-15","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/15/nigeria-strike","claim":"Reuters reported that an eight-month university lecturers' strike kept students out of classrooms, that the union suspended the strike in deference to presidential appeals, and that Buhari had said the government was dismayed and set aside 470 billion naira for salary enhancements in tertiary institutions.","locator":"article paragraphs 635-643","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting syndicated by Euronews, but still reporting a contentious labor dispute rather than a clean implementation record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 education-disruption evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Buhari because he appealed to end the strike and his budget response is cited, though the dispute also involved the union and universities.","contrary_evidence":["The union said the issues were not yet satisfactorily addressed, so the settlement remained incomplete."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In north-west Nigeria, cash grants take out-of-school children back to school","publisher":"UNICEF Nigeria","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://www.unicef.org/nigeria/stories/north-west-nigeria-cash-grants-take-out-school-children-back-school","claim":"UNICEF reported that cash grants helped return 300,000 pupils to school, including girls in Katsina, and that schools used grants for boreholes, toilets, classrooms, and learning materials while hundreds of girls were brought back into education.","locator":"story paragraphs around lines 75-100","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF story with concrete program outcomes and beneficiary testimony from 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 education access evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the broader Nigerian social-protection and education environment, but the program was implemented with UNICEF and local partners, not by Buhari alone.","contrary_evidence":["The story also says many children in the area remained out of school, so gains were partial and localized."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Strong collaboration for better health: Closing immunization gaps in Nigeria through a primary health care approach","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.who.int/about/accountability/results/who-results-report-2022-2023/country-story/2023/strong-collaboration-for-better-health--closing-immunization-gaps-in-nigeria-through-a-primary-health-care-approach","claim":"WHO reported that Nigeria ranked first among zero-dose countries in 2022 with over 2.3 million estimated zero-dose children, that the government prioritized reaching these communities in its national immunization and PHC strategy, and that data-targeted collaboration helped reach millions of children with immunization services.","locator":"country story paragraphs 260-270 and 278-280","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO results-report country story with target-year estimates and explicit system responses, albeit published retrospectively.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospectively describes 2022 health-access conditions and the 2020-2023 response period overlapping Buhari's tenure.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Buhari's administration through national strategy and PHC system oversight, but implementation was shared with WHO, UNICEF, and subnational actors.","contrary_evidence":["The same source shows a very large remaining immunization gap, so progress was incomplete."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across World Bank, IMF, NBS, WHO, UNICEF, WFP, State House, and Reuters-syndicated reporting.

Retained items were chosen because they add distinct 2022-relevant welfare evidence: budget priorities, actual service delivery, social protection, health coverage, WASH, floods, schooling, and distributional poverty data.

I rejected or de-emphasized:
- Duplicate World Bank landing pages that repeated the same poverty/public-spending message without adding new evidence.
- Generic country or programme pages that described ongoing work but did not supply a target-year claim.
- Out-of-period pages unless they clearly reported 2022 data or a 2022 policy action that shaped the year.
- Retrospective reports that did not add materially distinct 2022 evidence beyond the sources already retained.
- Purely promotional pages where the same claim was already captured in a more direct budget, survey, or evaluation source.

**Remaining gaps**
- Independent audit evidence on how much of the announced or reported social spending actually reached beneficiaries in 2022 is still thin.
- Direct national-level evidence on housing quality, sanitation upgrades, and nutrition delivery under Buhari in 2022 is limited.
- More third-party evaluation of BHCPF, NHIA enrollment, and school-feeding execution would improve attribution.
- Better causal evidence on whether federal decisions, as opposed to state or donor programs, drove the localized school-access gains would help.
- The welfare baseline is clear, but the record still mixes real delivery, partner-led programmes, and severe inherited deprivation.

Overall, the 2022 record shows real social-policy intent and some concrete delivery, especially on health coverage, social protection, and schooling, but it sits against very severe child poverty, weak WASH access, flood damage, and major education disruption.
